  "v85.qcd.valence.label": "Valence count: uud",
  "v85.qcd.dynamic.label": "QCD · quarks, antiquarks, gluons",
  "v85.qcd.mass.title": "2 · Rest energy of the whole system",
  "v85.qcd.mass.text": "Proton rest mass is about 938.27 MeV/c². The sum of elementary valence-quark masses is much smaller. Most of the rest is associated with QCD dynamics: quark motion, gluon fields and quantum interactions contribute to the bound system’s rest energy. Gluons have no rest mass but can carry energy and momentum; massless does not mean energy-free.",
  "v85.qcd.reference": "E₀ = M c² holds in the rest frame of the complete bound system. In general: M²c⁴ = E² − |p|²c². Contributions must not be treated as a simple sum of free-particle masses.",
  "v85.qcd.percent": "The often quoted 1%/99% comparison is a teaching-level order of magnitude for small elementary valence-quark masses relative to proton mass. It is not a universally unique division into quarks and gluons. Quantitative decompositions include sea quarks and anomaly contributions; individual contributions can depend on renormalization scheme and scale. “Energy” is not a separate substance constituting 99% of matter.",
  "v85.qcd.higgs": "The Higgs field is relevant to elementary quark masses. This does not contradict the dominant QCD contribution to proton mass: these are different statements about different levels of the same system.",
  "v85.qcd.gap.title": "3 · Mass gap: a different question",
  "v85.qcd.gap.text": "The Millennium problem asks for a mathematically rigorous construction of a nontrivial pure quantum Yang–Mills theory in four spacetime dimensions for every compact simple gauge group, with a positive energy gap Δ above the vacuum. Pure here means without additional quark fields. The mass gap is not a percentage of proton mass. QCD with light quarks is not identical to this pure theory.",
  "v85.qcd.status": "Source check: 11 November 2026. Clay lists the problem as unsolved. Experimentally determined proton masses and successful numerical QCD results are separate; they do not await that mathematical proof. A confinement or mass diagram does not solve the problem.",
  "v85.qcd.limit": "Knowledge article · no QCD solver or numerically calculated field configuration. The graphic is purely schematic. Physical hadron masses and the mathematical Yang–Mills problem are treated separately.",
